WEBVTT

00:00:01.50 --> 00:00:05.66
This is journey two of Hewn, the software factory: add a feature.

00:00:05.66 --> 00:00:08.99
Once a product exists, an Owner describes a feature,

00:00:08.99 --> 00:00:12.13
and Hewn runs only the stages that feature needs.

00:00:12.13 --> 00:00:17.07
It keeps every requirement number and journey number the product already has.

00:00:17.07 --> 00:00:21.87
This video walks through how it was built, from the form to the stage plan,

00:00:21.87 --> 00:00:25.20
the recorded decision, and the documents it changes.

00:00:26.35 --> 00:00:29.98
Journey two is for Owners whose product is already running.

00:00:29.98 --> 00:00:33.18
They should not have to start over to add one thing.

00:00:33.18 --> 00:00:38.04
They pick a product, describe the feature in ten to twenty thousand characters,

00:00:38.04 --> 00:00:39.09
and set a budget.

00:00:39.44 --> 00:00:44.51
A feature skips three stages every time: bootstrap, stack and scaffold.

00:00:44.51 --> 00:00:48.58
The repository, the stack and the solution already exist.

00:00:48.58 --> 00:00:52.22
If the agent decides the feature changes no screen,

00:00:52.22 --> 00:00:55.22
designs and design system are skipped too.

00:00:55.22 --> 00:00:59.36
The other stages run, limited to the new or changed scope.

00:01:00.51 --> 00:01:02.40
The Hewn API owns the plan.

00:01:02.40 --> 00:01:07.35
A new endpoint adds a feature direction to a project, with stages zero,

00:01:07.35 --> 00:01:09.38
four and six already skipped.

00:01:09.38 --> 00:01:13.49
The lease now tells the worker what the direction asks for,

00:01:13.49 --> 00:01:18.52
so every agent session names the feature and works only on what changes.

00:01:18.87 --> 00:01:20.90
One direction runs per project.

00:01:20.90 --> 00:01:25.55
A feature submitted while another direction is active waits in a queue.

00:01:25.55 --> 00:01:31.06
When the active direction completes, the orchestrator starts the oldest one waiting,

00:01:31.06 --> 00:01:32.37
first in, first out.

00:01:33.52 --> 00:01:37.41
Whether a feature changes the user interface is a judgement,

00:01:37.41 --> 00:01:39.87
and agents make judgements themselves.

00:01:39.87 --> 00:01:45.18
Stage one answers one fixed question in the decision log: does this feature change

00:01:45.18 --> 00:01:46.41
the user interface?

00:01:46.41 --> 00:01:50.95
The worker checks that the answer is there, commits it with the brief,

00:01:50.95 --> 00:01:52.63
and reports it to the API.

00:01:52.63 --> 00:01:57.81
With no change, the API marks designs and design system skipped before stage two

00:01:57.81 --> 00:01:58.59
would start.

00:01:59.74 --> 00:02:05.23
Requirement IDs and journey numbers appear in tests, pull request titles and videos,

00:02:05.23 --> 00:02:08.10
so a later direction must never change them.

00:02:08.10 --> 00:02:12.67
Before a stage's agent runs, the worker copies the numbered documents.

00:02:12.67 --> 00:02:15.68
Afterwards it checks that nothing was dropped,

00:02:15.68 --> 00:02:19.79
and that every new number comes after the highest existing one.

00:02:19.79 --> 00:02:25.02
An agent that renumbers a requirement fails the stage, and nothing is committed.

00:02:25.37 --> 00:02:31.31
Stage two follows the same rule for designs: it keeps every design in the manifest,

00:02:31.31 --> 00:02:34.32
adds new ones, and exports them all again.

00:02:34.32 --> 00:02:37.76
Stage nine plans only the designs not yet built,

00:02:37.76 --> 00:02:40.77
so the feature gets its own short backlog.

00:02:41.92 --> 00:02:45.38
Every slice started with failing acceptance tests.

00:02:45.38 --> 00:02:49.19
The backend gained thirteen tests against the real API,

00:02:49.19 --> 00:02:51.27
and all thirteen failed first.

00:02:51.27 --> 00:02:54.66
The worker gained eleven tests against fake APIs.

00:02:54.66 --> 00:02:58.96
All green, the backend has one hundred and thirty three tests,

00:02:58.96 --> 00:03:01.59
and the worker one hundred and twenty.

00:03:01.94 --> 00:03:04.78
Mission Control has twenty two unit tests.

00:03:04.78 --> 00:03:09.65
End to end, there are twelve behaviour tests, seven accessibility tests,

00:03:09.65 --> 00:03:12.49
three visual tests and six pipeline tests.

00:03:12.49 --> 00:03:17.96
Two of the pipeline tests run a real worker through a product and then a feature.

00:03:19.11 --> 00:03:20.50
Here is New direction.

00:03:20.50 --> 00:03:24.41
Choose feature for a product, and the form asks for a product,

00:03:24.41 --> 00:03:25.98
the feature and a budget.

00:03:25.98 --> 00:03:30.27
The plan on the right strikes through bootstrap, stack and scaffold.

00:03:30.62 --> 00:03:35.52
A feature that is too short gets an inline error, the same as a new product.

00:03:35.52 --> 00:03:38.87
A product that is still running says so in the list,

00:03:38.87 --> 00:03:41.00
and the feature queues behind it.

00:03:41.00 --> 00:03:45.13
The project page names the waiting direction and when it starts.

00:03:45.48 --> 00:03:47.90
Here is the real app with one worker.

00:03:47.90 --> 00:03:51.89
The Owner chooses feature for a product, picks Northwind Ops,

00:03:51.89 --> 00:03:55.49
describes a feature with no screen change, and submits.

00:03:55.49 --> 00:03:59.94
The skipped stages are struck through, and the rest run to complete.

00:04:00.29 --> 00:04:04.40
When it finishes, the project page says what was skipped and why,

00:04:04.40 --> 00:04:06.30
quoting the recorded decision.

00:04:06.30 --> 00:04:08.95
It works in dark mode and on a phone, too.

00:04:10.10 --> 00:04:14.21
This is what the feature changed in the product's repository.

00:04:14.21 --> 00:04:18.59
These documents come from the scripted agent the test stack uses,

00:04:18.59 --> 00:04:20.68
so they are deliberately plain.

00:04:20.68 --> 00:04:26.01
In the requirements, L2 one to three are untouched, and the feature is L2 four,

00:04:26.01 --> 00:04:27.42
the next free number.

00:04:27.77 --> 00:04:31.44
The user journeys are the same: journeys one to three are kept,

00:04:31.44 --> 00:04:33.60
and the feature becomes journey four.

00:04:33.95 --> 00:04:38.73
And this is the recorded decision that skipped designs and design system.

00:04:38.73 --> 00:04:42.39
When an agent renumbers an existing requirement instead,

00:04:42.39 --> 00:04:45.53
the stage fails with the requirement it dropped.

00:04:46.68 --> 00:04:48.30
Journey two is complete.

00:04:48.30 --> 00:04:53.62
A feature runs only the stages it needs, decides for itself whether the screens

00:04:53.62 --> 00:04:56.44
change, queues behind an active direction,

00:04:56.44 --> 00:04:59.61
and keeps every number the product already has.

00:04:59.61 --> 00:05:03.51
Next comes journey three, watching a direction as it runs.
